\fBcorrelation\fR
T}	T{
The cross\-listener window: the window length,\: how many addresses and facts are in it,\: how many facts the per\-address bound has pushed out and how many addresses were evicted,\: and what cluster peers have contributed. A window whose bounds are pushing facts out is one answering questions about the whole window from part of one,\: which this says rather than leaving it to be inferred
T}
T{
\fBtechniques\fR [\fB\-\:catalogue\fR]
T}	T{
What this daemon\(aqs refusals meant in MITRE ATT&CK for ICS terms: the technique,\: the tactic,\: MITRE\(aqs name for it and how often it was seen,\: most seen first. \fB\-\:catalogue\fR lists every technique this proxy can observe whether it has been seen or not,\: which is the honest form of a coverage answer \-\- a zero against a technique says "nothing tried",\: not "nothing detectable". The mapping from a refusal reason to a technique is in \fBdocs/\:ATTACK.\:md\fR
T}
T{
\fBsessions\fR [\fB\-\:kill\fR \fIID\fR] [\fB\-\:kill\-\:matching\fR \fB\-\:kind\fR \fIK\fR \fB\-\:listener\fR \fIL\fR \fB\-\:user\fR \fIU\fR]
T}	T{
The sessions the daemon is serving now \[u2014] SSH,\: SFTP,\: telnet,\: VNC,\: RDP,\: FTP and the Modbus device queues \[u2014] oldest first: the identifier,\: the kind,\: the listener,\: where the client came from,\: the login,\: the target it reached,\: one detail the kind chose (the desktop\(aqs name,\: the unit identifier,\: the subsystem) and how long it has been up. \fB\-\:kill\fR \fIID\fR closes one;\: \fB\-\:kill\-\:matching\fR closes every session matching the \fB\-\:kind\fR,\: \fB\-\:listener\fR and \fB\-\:user\fR given,\: and refuses to run with none of them,\: so a filter is never accidentally everything. A session is registered before its handshake finishes,\: so one stuck in a handshake is listed and can be closed. Every closure is written to the audit log with who asked. The names,\: logins and details came off the network and are clipped and filtered before they are printed. This is the live table;\: \fBsession\fR (singular) reads recordings back from disk
T}
T{
\fBsession\fR \fBlist\fR [\fB\-\:key\fR \fIREF\fR] \fIDIR\fR |\: \fBshow\fR [\fB\-\:safe\fR] [\fB\-\:input\fR] [\fB\-\:key\fR \fIREF\fR] \fIFILE\fR |\: \fBplay\fR [\fB\-\:speed\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-\:plain\fR] [\fB\-\:key\fR \fIREF\fR] \fIFILE\fR
T}	T{
Read a recorded gate session back. \fBlist\fR names the recordings in a directory with their size,\: start,\: dimensions and title;\: \fBshow\fR prints what the session showed,\: and \fBplay\fR replays it with its timing. The escape sequences that reach outside the window a replay is drawn in are never forwarded: a recording is the bytes a session sent,\: and a terminal is an interpreter of exactly those bytes,\: so writing one out unfiltered lets a recorded session set the reviewer\(aqs clipboard (OSC 52),\: retitle their window,\: or ask for a device report \[u2014] which the terminal answers on its own input,\: and a shell reads as a command line. \fBshow\fR keeps the text and drops every sequence;\: \fB\-\:safe\fR keeps the colours and cursor movement and writes the rest out inert;\: \fBplay\fR is \fB\-\:safe\fR by default and \fB\-\:plain\fR is the reading view. The file itself is never rewritten. A recording encrypted at rest (\fB*.\:cast.\:enc\fR,\: from \fBrecording.\:encryption\fR) needs \fB\-\:key\fR,\: which takes the same reference syntax as the configuration \[u2014] a path or \fBenv:\:NAME\fR,\: not a vault,\: since this command holds no vault configuration;\: \fBlist\fR shows both kinds and,\: without the key,\: marks the ones it cannot look inside rather than calling them unreadable
T}
